    "alert_id": "EX2_001",
    "title": "AKI likely pre-renal, not TDF toxicity",
    "message": "Guidelines state intercurrent illnesses with dehydration or sepsis may cause renal deterioration in patients on TDF, requiring creatinine checking during hospital admission. This patient has severe gastroenteritis with 5 days of vomiting and diarrhoea, hypotension (90/60), tachycardia, and dehydration signs. Creatinine rose sharply from 0.9 to 2.4 mg/dL. The urea-to-creatinine ratio is 24:1, strongly suggesting pre-renal AKI from volume depletion rather than intrinsic tubular toxicity. Urinalysis shows no protein, which also argues against TDF nephrotoxicity. Two family members share the illness, supporting infectious gastroenteritis as the cause. Permanent discontinuation of TDF may be unnecessary if renal function recovers with rehydration.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Rehydrate and recheck creatinine in 48–72 hours. If it recovers towards baseline, TDF may be continued. Avoid permanent TDF discontinuation for reversible pre-renal AKI.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 40, "chunk_id": "sahcs_adult_art_2024__sahcs_adult_art_2024__p40_c2"},
      {"page_number": 29, "chunk_id": "sahcs_adult_art_2024__sahcs_adult_art_2024__p29_c1"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "EX2_002",
    "title": "DTG creatinine rise does not fit this pattern",
    "message": "Guidelines describe DTG-related creatinine rises as small (<30 µmol/L), occurring within the first month of initiation, and non-progressive. This patient's creatinine rose by approximately 133 µmol/L (1.5 mg/dL) acutely after 2 years on DTG, which does not match the DTG-related pattern. The acute rise coincides with severe gastroenteritis and dehydration, pointing to pre-renal AKI rather than DTG effect. DTG inhibits tubular creatinine secretion without reducing GFR, producing small stable bumps, not acute spikes of this magnitude.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Do not attribute this acute creatinine rise to DTG. Continue DTG once vomiting is controlled; focus on volume resuscitation and treating the underlying gastroenteritis.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 7, "chunk_id": "sahcs_adult_art_2024__sahcs_adult_art_2024__p7_c1"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "EX2_003",
    "title": "If TDF must be stopped, use guideline alternative",
    "message": "Guidelines recommend ABC + 3TC + DTG as the alternative regimen when renal impairment develops due to TDF. If the clinician decides TDF must be discontinued, switching to ABC + 3TC + DTG preserves DTG and maintains viral suppression. However, given the pre-renal picture (high urea-to-creatinine ratio, dehydration, no proteinuria), TDF cessation may be premature. If TDF is held temporarily during AKI, ensure a clear plan for resumption or substitution once renal function is reassessed.",
    "issue_type": "plan_review",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "If TDF is discontinued, switch to ABC + 3TC + DTG. Reassess renal function after rehydration before making the change permanent.",
